COMPLIANT — the clause set satisfies the meta-rules it exists to enforce.

  - Every prescribed primitive was shown to work on the target platform, and the two
    that do NOT work are named as not working rather than quietly dropped. Clause 3
    identifies `timeout` as GNU coreutils and absent from a stock macOS, so it must be
    probed. Clause 4 records that `set -m` FAILS in zsh at every pole tested, with bash
    named as the positive control that proves the test itself works.
  - The clause ORDER is justified by what each layer buys: self-termination is clause 1
    because it is the only layer that makes correctness independent of everything else
    working.
  - Clause 5's mandated verification counts survivors and can print `CLEANUP FAILED`, so
    it is capable of the opposite verdict rather than being an unconditional string.
  - The BLOCKED corpus is quotable: each entry is a phrase an agent would actually write
    ("the confirmation line printed, so cleanup ran"), not an abstract description of a
    category of excuse.
  - The Wiring names a STRUCTURAL detector (the SessionStart/SessionEnd hook) rather than
    resting detection on the script's own output.

This pole deliberately shares its frontmatter, heading skeleton, clause count, Why lines,
DO/DO-NOT blocks, BLOCKED heading, Wiring and Origin with its violation twin. The two are
separable by the TRUTH of their platform claims and the ORDER of their priorities, never
by shape, length or furniture.
